//
// THIS FILE IS A PARTIAL PORT, AND THE BOUNDARY IS DELIBERATE. What is here is
// the whole of root's *managed-data* surface: the string, slice, map and
// interface object models, which together are the layouts every compiled Bit
// program manipulates on every line. What is NOT here is listed in "SEAMS"
// below — each was blocked on a named, still-unported dependency when this
// file was first written, and every one has since landed in a sibling file
// (see each entry for the ticket). A half-built subsystem would be worse than
// a missing one: the ABI is a contract, and a symbol that exists but is wrong
// is indistinguishable from a correct one until a program miscompiles.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE CONSTRAINTS THAT SHAPE THIS FILE (the complete list)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// 1. STATE IS MODULE STATE, NOT A CALLER-OWNED BLOCK. This is the first port to
//    use §11.11 module-level `let`, and it is the reason #1402 existed: root's
//    singletons ARE the globalness that #1362/#1363/#1364 each pushed out to
//    their caller. Every cell below is a scalar or a fixed array of scalars, so
//    rule 1 (the collector never scans module state) holds by construction —
//    and it must, because `heapBlock`'s free-list heads point into unmanaged
//    span memory that carries no object header.
//
// 2. TYPE DESCRIPTORS ARE BUILT AT RUNTIME, NOT AT COMPTIME. A module-state initializer must
//    be a compile-time constant and an array-typed `let` takes none (§11.11
//    rule 2), so the six descriptors are filled once by `rootInit`. Their
//    CONTENTS are identical field for field; only the moment of writing moves.
//    `refArrayInfo` in particular is matched by ADDRESS by the collector, so it
//    must be a stable cell — which module state is.
//
// 3. OUT-OF-MEMORY RETURNS 0 INSTEAD OF PANICKING. `@nosplit` cannot call
//    `panic` (it is an out-of-line call that can allocate and reach a
//    safepoint), the same constraint `runtime/chan` documents for send-on-closed
//    and `runtime/alloc` for its dropped assertions. Every allocating entry
//    point here returns 0 on exhaustion and bumps `statOom`, so exhaustion stays
//    VISIBLE (an assert would compile out of a release build; this does not)
//    rather than silent. Raising it as a real panic belongs to the boot layer
//    that owns the write path — see SEAM 3.
//
// 4. PAGES COME FROM A CALLER-SUPPLIED RESERVE. Unchanged from #1362/#1363 and
//    forced the same way: `syscall` is Linux-only, `extern function` is
//    Darwin-only, and both are rejected on the DECLARATION even when never
//    called, so a platform-free core cannot map its own pages. `rootBindMemory`
//    takes the reserve; binding a platform provider to it is SEAM 3's job.
//
// 5. ADDRESSES ARE SIGNED `int`. Bit's `int` is i64 (§11.1), as in every other
//    runtime module here. Userspace addresses on all three supported targets are
//    far below 2^63, so every comparison orders identically to an unsigned one.
//
// 6. `bit_rt_iface_as_ok`'s FLAG IS PROCESS-WIDE, NOT PER-THREAD. The natural
//    choice is a thread-local, but `@threadlocal` is rejected for Mach-O at emission (§11.11),
//    and aarch64-macos is a required target, so a per-thread cell cannot carry
//    it portably. This is SOUND ONLY UNDER THE ADJACENCY CONTRACT the ABI
//    already states for it (§2.2): lowering emits `iface_as` and `iface_as_ok`
//    back to back with no yield between, so no second assertion can land in the
//    window. It is nonetheless a WEAKER guarantee than a per-thread cell under
//    real multi-threaded mutation, and the honest fix is per-task scratch — see
//    SEAM 4. Flagged rather than hidden.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// SEAMS — what this file deliberately does not define, and where each one
// landed (#1681: this whole list was last revisited when SEAM 3's write half
// shipped; every remaining entry has since landed too, in the sibling files
// named below — what is left everywhere is #1369's archive swap, not new
// porting effort)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// SEAM 1 — THE NETWORK (12 symbols: `bit_rt_net_listen/local_port/accept/dial/
//   read/write/udp_bind/udp_send/udp_recv/udp_sender_host/udp_sender_port/
//   resolve`). LANDED, but not in this file: `runtime/net/` is ported in
//   `runtime/net/{linux,darwin}/netabi.bit` (#1366), reshaped per-platform by
//   #1600 after the platform-free-parent-plus-`extern` shape this paragraph
//   once assumed ("a mechanical half-hour" of marshalling in THIS file) tripped
//   the Linux `--emit-obj` E0078 gate. All 12 wrappers exist and
//   tests/imports/{nettcp,netudp,nethttp,nettcpbulk,netdns} pass against a
//   Bit-sourced archive. Nothing in this file references net, so nothing here
//   needed to change for the port to land.
//
//   THE NETPOLLER providers landed with #1364 (`runtime/sched/{linux,darwin}/
//   poll.bit`) and were WIRED INTO THE LIVE BOOT PATH by #1673 —
//   `runtime/root/<os>`'s `boot` creates the descriptor and binds it, and its
//   worker loop drains it — so a read that must wait parks and is woken.
//
// SEAM 2 — THE STOP-THE-WORLD REGISTRY (`bit_rt_gc_thread_enter/thread_exit/
//   blocking_begin/blocking_end`, and the safepoint handshake). LANDED:
//   `runtime/gc/`'s `World`/`Mutator` (runtime/gc/:176-350 — `claim`, `release`, `park`,
//   `stopRequested`, `beginBlocking`, `rendezvous`, `restart`,
//   `forEachParkedFrame`) — which `runtime/gc/gc.bit`'s #1363 collector-core
//   port deliberately left out — is ported in `runtime/gc/gcworld.bit` (#1575).
//   The four ABI symbols above are thin wrappers over exactly that registry
//   and are pinned there now (`bit_rt_root_gc_thread_enter` etc.), the same
//   placeholder convention every other ported pin in this tree uses.
//
// SEAM 3 — BOOT AND PROCESS ENTRY. LANDED. The write/exit half is in
//   `runtime/root/{linux,darwin}/io.bit`: `bit_rt_panic`, `bit_rt_assert`,
//   `bit_rt_print`, `bit_rt_eprint`, `bit_rt_os_exit` and `bit_rt_iface_assert`
//   (#1715 — the one-result type assertion's panic half, `../iface.bit`'s
//   `dynTypeOf` decides across the module boundary) (pinned with this
//   file's `bit_rt_root_` prefix, same rename obligation), over a shared
//   `writeAll`/`panicWrite` pair. Linux takes raw `write`/`exit_group` syscalls
//   (§11.8), Darwin libSystem `write`/`_exit` (§11.7), which is why they are two
//   files: the two primitives cannot appear in one module. `_start`/
//   `rtStartMain`/`boot` are `{linux,darwin}/boot.bit` (#1608); the kernel-facing
//   OS provider (`os_self_exe`/`os_run`/`os_run_test`) is `{linux,darwin}/os.bit`
//   (#1605); the fs provider is `{linux,darwin}/fs.bit` (#1606); the time
//   provider is `{linux,darwin}/time.bit` (#1604). Verified by
//   tests/stress/rootio{,linux,darwin} on all three targets.
//
//   A CONSTRAINT THE WRITE HALF SURFACED IS STILL LIVE, and #1369 must plan
//   around it: **nothing in a platform provider can be `@nosplit`, because
//   `@nosplit` rejects `syscall`** (E0075, measured). A freestanding archive
//   member must be `@nosplit` (§10.3), so as things stand the write path — and
//   therefore any fully-Bit `libbitrt.a` — cannot be freestanding. It is the
//   same shape as the `ptrOf` gap #1421 is fixing: §10.3.1 admits forms that
//   "lower to inline machine instructions rather than a call", and §11.8 says
//   the backends emit the kernel trap inline, so `syscall` meets §10.3.1's own
//   criterion and is simply missing from its allowlist. See `linux/io.bit`'s
//   header for the full argument. Every syscall-using provider in this tree is
//   non-`@nosplit` today, so this is a pre-existing property of the port, not a
//   regression from it.
//
// SEAM 4 — PER-GOROUTINE SLOTS (`bit_rt_set_err`/`get_err`,
//   `bit_rt_chan_recv_ok`). LANDED, split into `runtime/root/slots.bit`
//   (#1577): PER-TASK SCRATCH at the base of the running task's own stack —
//   the mechanism `runtime/sched`'s `schedCurrentTask` and `runtime/chan`'s
//   `scratchOf` establish, and strictly stronger than a per-OS-thread
//   thread-local (which Mach-O refuses at emission anyway) because it
//   survives task migration. The layout is `runtime/sched/scratch.bit`'s
//   `chanScratchWords` contract, shared with `runtime/chan`'s
//   `bit_rt_chan_recv` wrapper (#1581) so both name the same words.
//
// SEAM 5 — FLOAT TEXT AND THE TRANSCENDENTALS (`bit_rt_string_from_float`,
//   `bit_rt_parse_float`, `bit_rt_pow/atan2/log/log2/log10`). LANDED, split
//   into `runtime/root/floats.bit`/`floatspow.bit`/`floatsatan.bit`/
//   `floatslog.bit` (#1503 split the file out of this one; #1609 ported the
//   five transcendentals and the float<->bits primitives they needed). The
//   formatter is shortest-round-trip (Ryu-class) and the parser a
//   correctly-rounded decimal->binary conversion, so each is measured
//   bit-exact over the `bench/translog` corpus
//   (structural edges, every biased exponent both signs, a 500k xorshift
//   sweep, domain edges, NaN matched by class) and mutation-tested — a 1-ULP
//   drift here flips a `const_float` golden's printed string, so approximate
//   was never an option.
//
//   THE ROUNDING SIX (`floor`/`ceil`/`round`/`trunc`/`sqrt`/`float32Bits`)
//   turned out not to be the easy half: an earlier attempt split them off as
//   "one-liners over primitives that already lower to the instruction", but
//   they instead lowered to a call to the very symbol being ported — a
//   self-reference `tests/bit/rootpins.bit` catches. #1442 moved them into inline
//   IR ops instead (`prim_unary_ops`, both compilers); `floats.bit`'s SEAM 6
//   note has the full trap and is where that seam now lives, not in this file.

// SYMBOL PINS CARRY A `bit_rt_root_` PREFIX, NOT THE REAL ABI NAMES.
//
// `runtime/root/` is still linked into every binary and already defines
// `bit_rt_map_new`, `bit_rt_slice_get` and the rest; pinning those names here
// makes every link fail with `DuplicateSymbol` (measured, not predicted). The
// pins still have to exist — `-O1` splices callee bodies into their callers, so
// the freestanding emit (#1400) resolves by symbol and refuses on anything
// unpinned — so they are prefixed instead of dropped.
//
// #1369 OWNS THE RENAME: when it retires `runtime/root/`, each pin below drops its
// `_root` infix and claims the real ABI name. The prefix is a link-time
// placeholder, not a second ABI, and no other module should call these by name.
// `runtime/alloc` is pinned the same way for a different reason — its names
// were never in the ABI to begin with (ABI.md names no allocator symbol).

import { loadWord, storeWord, loadByte, storeByte, wordAt } from "../gc"
import { worldBind, worldWords } from "../gc"
import { worldBindSyscallRegs, maxMutators, muRegWords } from "../gc"
import {
  gcInit, gcBindMemory, gcConfigure, gcAlloc, gcAllocRaw, gcOwns, gcWords,
  gcTiSize, gcTiPtrOffsetsPtr, gcTiPtrOffsetsLen, gcTiNamePtr, gcTiNameLen,
  gcTiMethodsPtr, gcTiMethodsLen, gcHeaderSize, gcHdrInfo,
} from "../gc"
import { heapInit, heapWords } from "../alloc"
import { spinUnlocked } from "../"

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Object layouts (ABI.md §2) — reproduced field for field from `runtime/root/`
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// A `string` value: a GC object holding `{ptr, len}` immediately followed by its
// bytes, with `ptr` addressing those inline bytes. The object is therefore a
// LEAF — `ptr` is an interior pointer into the object itself, never a separate
// reference — which is why `stringInfo` carries no pointer offsets. This is also
// why `bit_rt_string_slice` COPIES instead of sharing: an interior pointer
// cannot keep a backing object alive (ABI.md §3).
export const strPtr: int = 0
export const strLen: int = 8
export const strHeaderSize: int = 16

// A `[]T` value: `{buf, len, off, cap, is_ref}`. `buf` is a SEPARATE GC object
// (the header's one traced reference, hence ptr_offsets = [0]) holding
// `off + cap` words; the view is `buf[off .. off+len]`. A reslice shares `buf`
// and only moves `off`/`len`/`cap`, so `buf` stays an object BASE and no
// interior pointer is ever stored as a reference. `len` sits at +8, the same
// offset as the string header's, so one `slice_len` serves both.
export const slcBuf: int = 0
export const slcLen: int = 8
export const slcOff: int = 16
export const slcCap: int = 24
export const slcIsRef: int = 32
export const slcHeaderSize: int = 40

// A `map<K,V>`: three parallel `cap`-slot buffers plus the bookkeeping. `keys`,
// `vals` and `ctrl` are all traced bases (ptr_offsets = [0, 8, 16]) — `ctrl` is
// a leaf buffer but must still be KEPT ALIVE, which only a traced slot does.
export const mapKeys: int = 0
export const mapVals: int = 8
export const mapCtrl: int = 16
export const mapLen: int = 24
export const mapCap: int = 32
export const mapUsed: int = 40
export const mapKeyIsString: int = 48
export const mapValIsRef: int = 56
export const mapHeaderSize: int = 64

// Per-slot state. Linear probing with tombstones; the 7/8 load-factor grow
// below guarantees an EMPTY slot always exists, which is what makes every probe
// loop terminate in at most `cap` steps.
const ctrlEmpty: int = 0
const ctrlFull: int = 1
const ctrlTomb: int = 2

// The initial slot count. Power of two, so `& (cap - 1)` is the modulus.
const mapInitCap: int = 8

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Process-wide singletons (§11.11) — runtime/root/'s `g_*`
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Every cell is a scalar or a fixed array of scalars: rule 1 holds, so the
// collector never scans any of it. That is REQUIRED here, not merely allowed —
// `heapBlock` holds 37 free-list heads pointing into unmanaged mmap span memory
// with no object header, and walking one as a reference would decode arbitrary
// bytes as a GC header.
//
// Sizes are literals because a module-state array's length must be a literal,
// not a `const` (measured: `let a: [N]i64` is E0021). `rootInit` asserts each
// against the exported width it stands for, so a drift in `gcWords`/`heapWords`
// fails loudly at boot instead of corrupting memory.
let gcState: [37]i64          // gcWords
let heapBlock: [39]i64        // heapWords

// The World/Mutator registry (`gcworld.bit`, `worldWords` = 1285) — `runtime/root/`'s
// `g_world`. Owned HERE for the same reason `gcState` is: `runtime/gc` is
// `@nosplit` throughout and `@nosplit` rejects `ptrOf` (E0075), so the block and
// its address must come from a module that can take one. `rootInit` hands it to
// `worldBind`; until that call every `gcworld` wrapper is a safe no-op and the
// safepoint poll returns without ever touching a registry — which is exactly
// what #1639 measured, the collector dormant because nothing bound it.
//
// Zeroed by construction, which is the initial state the registry wants (every
// slot free, no stop pending, epoch 0).
// 1285 = maxMutators * muWords + 5, up from 1029 when #1904 gave each slot one
// more word (`muSp`, the syscall state's published stack pointer).
let worldBlock: [1285]i64     // worldWords

// #1904's saved callee-saved register file, `muRegWords` words per mutator slot,
// owned here for the same reason `worldBlock` is: `runtime/gc` is `@nosplit`
// throughout and `@nosplit` rejects `ptrOf` (E0075).
//
// A SEPARATE BLOCK, NOT MORE WORDS PER SLOT — a cost decision, and it used to
// claim to be more than that. The note here said folding these 12 words into the
// mutator slot makes `worldBlock` 4357 words and "every Bit binary then SIGSEGVs
// under the collector". It does not, and #1991 closed on why: the crash was
// `tests/stress/gcworld` binding its own GC-heap slice as the process-wide
// registry, and `worldWords` sized that slice too, so a test bug read as a limit
// on this array. `worldBlock: [4357]i64` with `muWords = 17` was then measured
// green. The blocks stay separate because 12 words x 256 slots for a state only
// a syscall-parked thread uses is a poor trade, not because the alternative
// crashes.
let syscallRegBlock: [3072]i64   // maxMutators * muRegWords
let heapLockCell: i32 = 0
let listLockCell: i32 = 0

// The six type descriptors, plus the two pointer-offset arrays they name.
// See `rootInit` for the layout; 7 words is one `TypeInfo` (ABI.md §2).
let infoBlock: [46]i64

const riString: int = 0
const riSlice: int = 7
const riSliceBuf: int = 14
const riMap: int = 21
const riMapCtrl: int = 28
const riRefArray: int = 35
const riSliceOffs: int = 42   // [0]
const riMapOffs: int = 43     // [0, 8, 16]
const riWords: int = 46

// The addresses of `gcState` and `infoBlock`, taken ONCE by `rootInit`.
//
// THIS INDIRECTION IS FORCED, and the reason matters. `ptrOf` is REJECTED in a
// `@nosplit` body (E0075, measured) even on module state, which SPEC §11.11
// explicitly says is legal there ("`ptrOf` yields its address for the atomic
// builtins ... legal inside a `@nosplit` body"). Filed as a spec/implementation
// mismatch rather than worked around silently.
//
// And `@nosplit` is NOT optional for the bodies below, it is the correctness
// property: they hold live object addresses in plain `int` locals, which the
// stack maps do NOT describe as references (ABI.md §4 names only reference-typed
// slots). A safepoint firing inside `rtStringConcat` would therefore find no
// root for the string it has just allocated and sweep it. `@nosplit` is what
// guarantees no safepoint lands there — so the addresses must be reachable
// WITHOUT `ptrOf`, i.e. as plain module-state reads plus `wordAt`.
let gcAddr: i64 = 0
let infoAddr: i64 = 0

let booted: i64 = 0

// The `ok` of the most recent `bit_rt_iface_as`. Process-wide — see constraint 6.
let ifaceOk: i64 = 0

// Allocation failures observed since boot. Aborting on OOM would be the usual
// choice, but `@nosplit` cannot abort, so it is counted instead and every
// allocating entry point returns 0.
// A non-zero value means some caller got a null it may not have checked.
let statOom: i64 = 0

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Boot
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Writes one `TypeInfo` (ABI.md §2) at word `at` of `infoBlock`.
//
// `name` is carried as an address/length pair, never a NUL-terminated string; a descriptor
// with no name passes 0/0. Names are only ever read by
// `bit_rt_iface_assert`'s message, so a nameless descriptor degrades that
// message rather than misbehaving.
@nosplit function writeInfo(base: int, at: int, size: int, offsAddr: int, offsLen: int, nameAddr: int, nameLen: int) {
  let w = base + at * 8
  storeWord(w + gcTiSize, size)
  storeWord(w + gcTiPtrOffsetsPtr, offsAddr)
  storeWord(w + gcTiPtrOffsetsLen, offsLen)
  storeWord(w + gcTiNamePtr, nameAddr)
  storeWord(w + gcTiNameLen, nameLen)
  storeWord(w + gcTiMethodsPtr, 0)
  storeWord(w + gcTiMethodsLen, 0)
}

// Has this process's root state already been initialised (#1835)?
//
// `rootInit` returning false is not a usable answer to this, because it is only
// false AFTER the caller has already called `rootBindMemory` — and that call
// rebinds the live collector's heap out from under a running process. A program
// that wants to know whether it is the one booting the runtime has to ask
// BEFORE it touches anything.
//
// The caller this exists for is a self-driving stress program. Once the Bit
// runtime is the live one (G3), `boot` has already run `rootInit`, so such a
// program must use the live root rather than stand up a private one — the same
// split Go draws, where a component owning process-global state is tested
// against the live runtime instead of a second instance. `runtime/stw` got the
// other half of that split (#1836): its state IS a bound address, so it could be
// parameterised. Root's is module-level arrays that ARE the singletons, so it
// cannot, and asking is what is left.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_booted") function rootBooted(): bool {
  return booted != 0
}

// Binds the memory the collector carves its heap out of. Separate from
// `rootInit` for the same reason `gcBindMemory` is separate from `gcInit`: the
// pages come from a platform provider this core cannot import (constraint 4).
// MUST be called before `rootInit`.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_bind_memory")
function rootBindMemory(spanAddr: int, spanBytes: int, idxAddr: int, idxBytes: int, pageSize: int) {
  let g = ptrOf(gcState)
  gcAddr = int(g)
  infoAddr = int(ptrOf(infoBlock))
  gcBindMemory(g, spanAddr, spanBytes, idxAddr, idxBytes, pageSize)
}

// Boots the allocator, the collector and the type descriptors, in that order.
//
// THE ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING AND MATCHES `boot()` EXACTLY: the heap is
// initialized before the collector, because `gcInit` takes the heap's address
// and its two lock cells; the descriptors are written before the first
// allocation, because `gcAlloc` reads a descriptor's size to size the body.
// A "cleaner" order — descriptors first, say — happens to work today and would
// break the moment a descriptor is allocated rather than static. Reproduced
// rather than improved, per the port's standing rule.
//
// Returns false if the collector could not start; true otherwise. Calling it
// twice is refused — there is no such thing as rebooting this runtime.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_init")
function rootInit(markStackLen: int): bool {
  if (booted != 0) {
    return false
  }
  // A drift in either width would silently truncate the block these arrays
  // stand in for. Literal lengths cannot reference the consts, so check them.
  if (gcWords != 37 || heapWords != 39 || worldWords != 1285) {
    return false
  }

  gcAddr = int(ptrOf(gcState))
  infoAddr = int(ptrOf(infoBlock))

  let hp = ptrOf(heapBlock)
  heapInit(hp)
  let hlk = ptrOf(heapLockCell)
  atomicStore(hlk, spinUnlocked)
  let llk = ptrOf(listLockCell)
  atomicStore(llk, spinUnlocked)

  let base = infoAddr

  // Pointer-offset arrays, written before the descriptors that name them.
  storeWord(base + riSliceOffs * 8, 0)
  storeWord(base + riMapOffs * 8 + 0, 0)
  storeWord(base + riMapOffs * 8 + 8, 8)
  storeWord(base + riMapOffs * 8 + 16, 16)

  // `string` and the two element buffers are LEAVES; the three headers carry
  // the traced offsets. Sizes are the header sizes: a string's inline bytes and
  // a buffer's words are sized per allocation via `gcAllocRaw`, which is what
  // the 0-size leaf descriptors here mean.
  writeInfo(base, riString, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)
  writeInfo(base, riSlice, slcHeaderSize, base + riSliceOffs * 8, 1, 0, 0)
  writeInfo(base, riSliceBuf, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)
  writeInfo(base, riMap, mapHeaderSize, base + riMapOffs * 8, 3, 0, 0)
  writeInfo(base, riMapCtrl, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)
  // The `ref_array_info` sentinel: matched by ADDRESS by the collector, never
  // by content, which is why it must be a stable module-state cell.
  writeInfo(base, riRefArray, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)

  let g = ptrOf(gcState)
  if (!gcInit(g, int(hp), int(hlk), int(llk), base + riRefArray * 8, markStackLen)) {
    return false
  }

  // Publish the World registry (`runtime/root/`'s `g_world`, a plain global there).
  // AFTER `gcInit`, so a thread that registers the instant this returns finds a
  // collector that is already up.
  worldBind(int(ptrOf(worldBlock)))
  worldBindSyscallRegs(int(ptrOf(syscallRegBlock)))

  booted = 1
  return true
}

// The address of the collector state this module owns, for the one caller that
// needs it and cannot reach it any other way: `boot` binds the three blocks a
// stop-the-world enumerates roots from (`stwBind`, `runtime/stw`), and the
// collector block is this module's. Not the collector's ABI surface — every
// other user of `gcState` goes through the entry points above.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_gc_addr") function rootGcAddr(): int {
  let a = gcAddr
  return a
}

// The World registry's address, for the same caller and the same reason as
// `rootGcAddr` above: `stwBind` also caches this one (#1700), so stwPoll's hot
// path can read it without a cross-module call to `worldBlockAddr`.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_world_addr") function rootWorldAddr(): int {
  let a = int(ptrOf(worldBlock))
  return a
}

// `BIT_GC` / `BIT_GC_STATS` equivalents. Split out because reading the
// environment needs the process entry (SEAM 3); the values are handed in.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_configure")
function rootConfigure(enabled: bool, stress: bool, minTrigger: int, growthPct: int, verbose: bool) {
  let g = ptrOf(gcState)
  gcConfigure(g, enabled, stress, minTrigger, growthPct, verbose)
}

// Allocation failures since boot. Zero on any run that did not exhaust memory;
// see constraint 3.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_stat_oom")
function rootStatOom(): int {
  return statOom
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Allocation
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// `bit_rt_gc_alloc` (ABI.md §2/§6): a zeroed body of the type `infoAddr`
// describes. 0 on exhaustion (constraint 3).
//
// The calling thread is registered as a mutator here too, because the
// allocation door is reached long before the first safepoint and an
// unregistered allocator is one the rendezvous does not wait for. That
// registration is SEAM 2 (`World`, runtime/gc/gcworld.bit) and is landed:
// gcAlloc/gcAllocRaw (runtime/gc/gcalloc.bit) both call registerAllocator()
// as their first statement, so it happens on every call through here.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_gc_alloc")
function rtGcAlloc(info: int): int {
  let g = wordAt(gcAddr)
  let body = gcAlloc(g, info)
  if (body == 0) {
    statOom = statOom + 1
  }
  return body
}

// A `len`-byte string body: the `{ptr, len}` header followed by the bytes, with
// `ptr` addressing them. Returns the object address, or 0 on exhaustion.
//
// PINNED because `strings.bit`'s exported wrapper `rtStrAlloc`
// (`bit_rt_port_root_str_alloc`) is a one-liner: at `-O1` the inliner splices
// that body into its cross-module callers (`runtime/rand/{linux,darwin}`,
// `runtime/net/{linux,darwin}`), so the reference that survives into their
// objects is THIS callee, not the wrapper — and an unpinned callee is exactly
// what `--freestanding` refuses (#1627, SPEC §11.9). Pinning the wrapper alone
// does not protect an inlinable wrapper's callee. Port-internal
// (`bit_rt_port_root_`, not `bit_rt_root_`): ABI.md names no allocator symbol,
// so this is not a future ABI name and #1369's rename must not touch it — same
// convention as the neighbouring `bit_rt_port_root_alloc_buf`.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_root_alloc_string")
function allocString(nbytes: int): int {
  let g = wordAt(gcAddr)
  let base = infoAddr
  let body = gcAllocRaw(g, strHeaderSize + nbytes, base + riString * 8)
  if (body == 0) {
    statOom = statOom + 1
    return 0
  }
  storeWord(body + strPtr, body + strHeaderSize)
  storeWord(body + strLen, nbytes)
  return body
}

// A `cap`-word element buffer. A ref buffer carries the `ref_array_info`
// descriptor so the collector traces its words as roots (ABI.md §2); a non-ref
// buffer is a leaf. `cap == 0` yields a valid zero-length body — never indexed,
// because every bounds check rejects it.
//
// EXPORTED FOR `runtime/chan` (SEAM 3's obligation to #1581). `bit_rt_chan_make`
// needs a `cap`-word channel ring and `bit_rt_select_alloc` needs an `n*4`-word
// case buffer, and BOTH must be TRACED when they carry references (a `chan<T>`
// or `select` case whose `T` is a reference type parks across a collection with
// the only live copy of a received value sitting in this buffer — ABI.md §11).
// The `ref_array_info` sentinel that makes that tracing exact is root's own
// (`infoBlock[riRefArray]`, handed to `gcInit` by `rootInit`) and is private to
// this file, so the allocator that selects it must live here and be exposed
// rather than reimplemented in chan against a descriptor it cannot name. The
// companion process-lifetime allocator a channel HANDLE needs (runtime/root/'s
// `page_allocator.create`) is `runtime/alloc`'s already-exported `mapPages`, so
// nothing further is owed here. The pin is PORT-INTERNAL (`bit_rt_port_root_`,
// not `bit_rt_root_`): ABI.md names no allocator symbol, so this is not a future
// ABI name — it exists only because `-O1` splices bodies across the chan/root
// module boundary and the freestanding emit resolves the splice by symbol. Same
// convention as `linux/io.bit`'s `bit_rt_port_root_io_write_all`.
//
// PRECONDITION unchanged from every allocating entry point here: `rootInit` must
// have run (`gcAddr`/`infoAddr` set). A channel is only ever made after boot, so
// the contract holds by construction.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_root_alloc_buf")
function allocBuf(words: int, isRef: bool): int {
  let g = wordAt(gcAddr)
  let base = infoAddr
  let info = base + riSliceBuf * 8
  if (isRef) {
    info = base + riRefArray * 8
  }
  let body = gcAllocRaw(g, words * 8, info)
  if (body == 0) {
    statOom = statOom + 1
  }
  return body
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Strings (ABI.md §2)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Copies `n` bytes from `src` to `dst`. Ascending, and the two regions here are
// always disjoint (a fresh object and an existing one), so no overlap rule is
// needed. Bounded by `n`.
@nosplit function copyBytes(dst: int, src: int, n: int) {
  let i = 0
  while (i < n) {
    let b = loadByte(src + i)
    storeByte(dst + i, b)
    i = i + 1
  }
}

// THE STRING FUNNEL, AND THE NULL-HEADER CONTRACT (#1564/#1570).
//
// A zeroed struct's `string` field is the NULL WORD (SPEC §13.4), so every
// string entry point can be handed 0 by perfectly ordinary Bit code. `runtime/root/`
// answers that with one `strBytes(s: ?*const RtBytes)` helper returning an empty
// byte view, and every reader above it is written against the view rather than
// the header. These two are that helper, split in the shape the port already
// used: a null header has NO BYTES and LENGTH ZERO, so it reads as `""`.
//
// FUNNELLING IS THE POINT, not an optimisation. Every string reader in this
// module — `rtStringConcat/Eq/Byte/Slice`, `rtBytesFromString`, `rtParseFloat`,
// `rtOsEnv`, `fsPathZ`, `mapHash`/`mapKeyEq` — goes through exactly this pair,
// so the guard cannot be forgotten at a new call site the way a sprinkled
// `if (s == 0)` would be. `strBytes` returning 0 is safe precisely because
// `strSize` returns 0 with it: every consumer is a loop or copy bounded by the
// length, so the null address is never dereferenced.
//
// A NULL POINTER IS NOT HANDED BACK AS A HEADER, deliberately — no shared empty
// string object exists anywhere in this port. See `rtSliceAppend` for why a
// process-wide empty would be actively unsafe for slices.
@nosplit function strBytes(s: int): int {
  if (s == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  let p = loadWord(s + strPtr)
  return p
}

@nosplit function strSize(s: int): int {
  if (s == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  let n = loadWord(s + strLen)
  return n
}
